{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>A chosen [thing], wealth</b> — two joined [notions]. 'Good' stands in place of both, and likewise: <b>A good name is chosen over great wealth</b> (Proverbs 22:1), for <b>a rich man and a poor man meet</b> (Proverbs 22:2), and its meaning is that the causes descend from the heavens upon the earth, and the people who receive them meet with them — that is, that this one encounters wealth and that one encounters poverty." "And YHWH does all of this — that He gives the wealth and the poverty, and He makes the rich man and the poor man, but a good name is not a פגע, for a person acquires it for himself through his good deeds, and חן, that he finds favor in the eyes of all who see him, is upon [account of] the good deed. Therefore they are chosen over great wealth."], :book :proverbs, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 22, :notes {0 "פגע read here as an adjectival noun: a 'thing encountered,' i.e., something that comes upon a person from without, in contrast to a good name which one acquires through one's own deeds."}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Proverbs -- Daat" "http://www.daat.ac.il/he-il/tanach/parshanut_hamikra/full-text/eben-ezra/mishley/"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T19:24:09.949498427Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}